permalink: /robots.txt
layout: false
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#- Crawlers read robots.txt ONLY at the root of an address (RFC 9309; Google: "crawlers don't
    check for robots.txt files in subdirectories"). This file is the live one
    only with a custom domain — which the site has since November 2026 (grapevine.neta65.org,
    README §12, "Using your own address"): the site is the root of its address, so THIS file is
    the root robots.txt search engines read, and the Weekly link check says "Skipped" for it
    (scripts/ops/root_robots.py).
    Built as a GitHub Pages PROJECT site (https://neta65.github.io/aagrapevine/ — the fallback
    address of the Code check and of a build on a computer) it is published at
    /aagrapevine/robots.txt instead, where no search engine reads it. The file that counts there
    is https://neta65.github.io/robots.txt, and only the NETA65 account's own user-site
    repository, NETA65/neta65.github.io, can publish it: its ready-to-upload files are in
    docs/user-site/, the owner's steps in README → "Search engines" (step C), and the Weekly link
    check reads it every Sunday for such an address. Without that repository the root answers
    404, which means "crawl everything".
    So keep it as it is — everything allowed (Google needs the CSS, scripts and JSON files to
    show the pages), and the sitemap. Every search engine and every AI search or answer tool
    (Googlebot, Bingbot, OAI-SearchBot and ChatGPT-User, Claude-SearchBot and Claude-User,
    PerplexityBot, Applebot, DuckAssistBot …) follows the "User-agent: *" group: naming one of
    them would only replace that group for it.
    The ONE exception (since round 35) is the committee's own choice about AI-model TRAINING,
    config/site.yml search_engines.ai_training (README → "Search engines", step D):
      "allow"     (the default) the three lines below, nothing else;
      "disallow"  + one group per training token, each "Disallow: /" — the shared training signal
                  "__usage_ai-training" (an IETF AI-preferences draft, October 2026: not a crawler and
                  not a crawl rule, only "do not train on this"; Anthropic reads it now, Microsoft
                  from February 1, 2027, Google from early 2027, as a Google-Extended "no") and the
                  training crawlers and tokens of today: GPTBot (OpenAI), ClaudeBot (Anthropic),
                  Google-Extended (Google's training and Gemini apps' grounding — never Search, AI
                  Overviews or AI Mode), Applebot-Extended (Apple), CCBot (Common Crawl, the open
                  crawl many models are trained on) and meta-externalagent (Meta). Named groups replace
                  "*" only for those names, so the search crawlers and the AI search ones stay
                  allowed either way (tests/test_seo_robots.py checks both settings with Python's own
                  robots.txt reader). Never a Disallow line for anything else;
      anything else counts as "allow" (eleventy/seo-check.js gives a build warning).
    The Sitemap line needs an absolute URL: site.url comes from config/site.yml (or the
    SITE_URL the GitHub Action sets). Without one it is left out. -#}
{%- set aiTraining = (((site.search_engines.ai_training if site.search_engines else "") or "allow") | string | trim | lower) -%}
{%- set trainingBots = ["GPTBot", "ClaudeBot", "Google-Extended", "Applebot-Extended", "CCBot", "meta-externalagent"] -%}
User-agent: *
Allow: /
{% if aiTraining == "disallow" %}

# No AI-model training, please (the committee's choice). Search engines and AI search
# answers may still read every page: they follow the group above.
User-agent: __usage_ai-training
Disallow: /
{% for bot in trainingBots %}

User-agent: {{ bot }}
Disallow: /
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
{% if site.url %}
Sitemap: {{ site.url }}/sitemap.xml
{% endif %}
